MEMORY

What you work out in one session is gone by the next unless you remember it. A
memory is a key and a body. Body is prose or code: one slot, both kinds,
because in this language they are the same material.

- (memory/remember key body) store one. Prose in the body is something to know.
  Quoted form in the body is something to do, a recipe you run later. Quote it:
  arguments are evaluated, so an unquoted form stores its result instead.
- (memory/recall query) retrieve by key or body text, strongest first. Query is
  words, not a pattern: every word that carries meaning must land somewhere in
  the one memory. Spelling is forgiven, a prefix counts. What it finds loads
  into your context, so recalling IS reading. No echo step to write after it.
- (memories) every memory as key, score and trigger, strongest first.
- (memory/forget key) drop one for good.
- (memory/forget-all) drop every memory, only when the user asks for a clean slate.
- (memory/replay key) evaluate a memory whose body is a form, return what it
  produced. A memory holding a defun installs that function on replay. This is
  how a remembered recipe becomes a running one.
- (memory/revise key body) correct a memory. See the window below.

Names are slashed because remember, recall and forget are ordinary English
verbs, and a bare one in an aside would be read as code.

FIRING BY ITSELF

With :on a memory watches for an event and loads itself when it happens,
without you asking. Trigger is a quoted form of a kind and a pattern. The
eight kinds:

  call     a form about to be evaluated, at any depth, so it catches a call
           nested inside a setq or a let
  result   the printed value a top-level form returned
  error    an evaluation that raised, so a past mistake surfaces exactly as it
           is about to repeat
  step     every step
  prose    words in the prose around your forms
  user     words in what the user just said
  recall   another memory being retrieved
  start    the user's first message, once, with no pattern: written (start)

For call the pattern is A FORM, written the way the call itself is written,
matched structurally. This gives precision about arguments:

(call (load-mcp "acme")) only that server, not every load
(call (load-mcp)) any call to load-mcp, whatever its arguments
(call (load-mcp _)) _ matches one argument, whatever it is
(call (acme/.*)) a head is a regular expression, so one
                                   pattern hooks every tool on a server
(call (setq _ (acme/get _))) the shape nests as deeply as you write it

A pattern list matches a PREFIX of the call: naming fewer arguments is looser,
naming more is tighter. Every other kind matches text. Its pattern is a string
used as a regular expression, matched case-insensitively anywhere in the text:

(user "file.*") whatever the user says about file.md, file.pdf or any other one
(user "^deploy") only what the user opens with the word

A pattern that is not a regular expression is read as plain text instead, so a
literal with a stray bracket still matches.

Combine patterns with all, any-of and not, in either sort of trigger:

(call (all (load-mcp) (not (load-mcp "acme"))))
(error (any-of "undefined" "void variable"))

Memory on a call to the loader surfaces what you know about a server the moment
you load it. Memory on an error surfaces the fix the moment the same error comes
back.

A start memory rides the user's first message and stays with it for the rest of
the conversation, so your role and your goal belong there. It loads once:
revise it and only the next conversation reads the new body.

A fired memory arrives as a line of REPL feedback, to you alone. It is not run
and the user does not see it. To run one, replay it. To show the user
something, echo it.

CHAINING

Retrieval is itself an event, so a memory can hook another memory and the two
arrive together. Say it outright with :links, which takes a list of keys.

It also happens on its own: memories that fire in the same step get wired to
each other, so things you keep using together start arriving together.

STRENGTH AND FORGETTING

Every memory has a score. Recalling one strengthens it. Leaving it alone lets
it fade, half its strength a week, and under a floor it is swept away. What you
keep using stays; what you wrote once and never needed goes. Nothing needs
tidying by hand, though memory/forget is there when something is wrong.

THE WINDOW

Retrieving a memory opens it: until the end of the step you can revise its body
to correct what it says. Outside that window revise is an error telling you to
recall it first. Reading and rewriting are one act on purpose: you fix a memory
while looking at it and seeing it was wrong.

GOTCHAS

- Memory body must be prose or a form that can be read back. A closure, a
  promise or a secret is a live value, prints in angle brackets, and remember
  rejects it. Store the quoted form that builds the thing, never the built
  thing itself.
- Quote a code body. Without the quote the form is evaluated and you store its
  result.
- memory/recall loads what it finds into your context. Do not follow it with an
  echo to see it properly; it is already in front of you.
- Firing does not run anything. A memory that fires on a call tells you what it
  knows; if it holds a recipe, replay it.
- Triggers watch the code you write into the REPL. A form evaluated inside a
  replay or inside a function body is not a step of its own and fires nothing.
- Memories outlive the interpreter. Definitions die on a reset, memories do
  not. A recipe worth keeping belongs in a memory, not in a defun you will lose.
- A memory with no :on never arrives by itself. It waits for a recall that names
  it. If you cannot guess its key later it is as good as lost. Storing a
  correction without a trigger is the commonest way to write a memory that
  never helps again.

RULES

- REMEMBER THE MOMENT A GUESS FAILS. A rejected call, a name that turned out
  wrong, an argument order you had backwards: that is the one thing worth
  keeping. The moment you know it is the only moment you will have the
  correction in front of you. Write it before moving on, while the right answer
  is on screen.
- GIVE IT THE TRIGGER THAT WILL REPEAT, not a description of the topic. Ask
  what you will be doing when you next need this, and hook that. A tool you
  misnamed belongs on the call that loads its server, so it arrives before you
  can misname it again. A mistake the interpreter rejected belongs on that
  error. A memory you must remember to look for is one you will not look for.
- NAME THE ARGUMENT THAT MADE IT TRUE. What you learned about one server is not
  true of the next, so (call (load-mcp "acme")) is right where
(call (load-mcp)) would fire on every server you ever load and teach you the
  wrong thing about most of them. Hook the whole call, not only the name.
- Remember the correction, not the transcript. "browser_navigate, not navigate"
  is worth keeping; the whole tool listing is not, and the next listing will
  differ anyway.
- Do not remember what the REPL can tell you. Anything doc prints, anything a
  discovery call returns, anything you can recompute: look it up instead.
  Memory is for what you had to find out the hard way.
- Recall before you guess. About to write a name you are not sure of, and the
  session has not told you? That is the cheapest possible recall.
